  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Omer Courtney Darr grew up in Washington state and learned to sail on Puget Sound and the Columbia River. He served in the US Navy during the Second World War, seeing action in the Aleutians, Philippines and Okinawa. After leaving the service, he bought the schooner Nordlys and sailed her from Maryland to Tahiti, turning a wartime ambition into a working life at sea.\n\nDarr acquired the neglected Te Vega in 1954, rebuilt her in California and established an ambitious luxury passenger service linking Hawaii, Tahiti and other distant destinations. He later chartered and captained Wanderer on further South Pacific voyages, combining ownership, navigation and hands-on hospitality. Known for seamanship rather than publicity, he settled for many years on Moorea and remained closely associated with Pacific schooners until his death in Honolulu in 1999.",
    "html": "<p>Omer Courtney Darr grew up in Washington state and learned to sail on Puget Sound and the Columbia River. He served in the US Navy during the Second World War, seeing action in the Aleutians, Philippines and Okinawa. After leaving the service, he bought the schooner Nordlys and sailed her from Maryland to Tahiti, turning a wartime ambition into a working life at sea.</p>\r\n<p>Darr acquired the neglected Te Vega in 1954, rebuilt her in California and established an ambitious luxury passenger service linking Hawaii, Tahiti and other distant destinations. He later chartered and captained Wanderer on further South Pacific voyages, combining ownership, navigation and hands-on hospitality. Known for seamanship rather than publicity, he settled for many years on Moorea and remained closely associated with Pacific schooners until his death in Honolulu in 1999.</p>\r\n",
